#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175707 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "f2597771-4f5e-5a90-9c39-d8b902690340" :question/math-academy-id "e-568" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 2t + 3t^2.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=3?$" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={({t}^{2}+{t}^{3})|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}+{3}^{3}]-[{1}^{2}+{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =[9+27]-[1+1] \\\\  & =36-2 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=3$ is $34.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "15fea8a5-1937-5f81-80f3-3bc88a51bec6" :question/math-academy-id "e-7128" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^2 +4t.$ At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-20.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=4?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=4$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in[2,4].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(4) = x(2) + \\int_2^4 v(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(4) & =-20+{\\int }_{2}^{4}(6{t}^{2}+4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =-20+{[2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-20+([2(4)^{3}+2(4)^{2}]-[2(2)^{3}+2(2)^{2}]) \\\\  & =-20+([128+32]-[16+8]) \\\\  & =-20+(160-24) \\\\  & =-20+136 \\\\  & =116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=4$ is $x=116.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3a9d8a-a877-574d-86ef-be3ca3f5b61e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7129" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014400/assets/900988cb8ec54abcafc256a34c685829d2f5f00cdca373ad84dfa0ef70cc3957.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=2$ the particle is located at the position $x=-1.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=8?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t = 8$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,8].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(8) = x(2)+ \\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=-1$, and we can compute the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=8.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n\\int_2^8 v(t) \\, \\textrm dt = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(8) = -1+ 6 = 5\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=8$ is $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb5b1566-17c3-58b8-b00f-6cda1cf93271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292175} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb5ec3c-ac4f-533f-81f2-1fdb1ed88d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292176} {:answer/id #uuid "5332815c-36c3-58d9-991f-722f4ba2c53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292177} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1036c0-52e5-5d1c-8c38-60f8e5543902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292178} {:answer/id #uuid "92a786cd-fd48-5d59-bc9a-24358582507e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5332815c-36c3-58d9-991f-722f4ba2c53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292177} :answer-field/id #uuid "cea03fbc-b338-53c9-a25a-30131b5aa091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1052200-d76a-59c5-989a-4d2d76d89a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121007" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ the particle is located at the position $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}+{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4dd399-17ba-5d5d-a947-9f9d97c58fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174727} {:answer/id #uuid "91d2df24-cd40-51a6-ad1c-36ace1984565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174728} {:answer/id #uuid "078faf97-fccc-5d7d-b441-d5f5a91ba9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174729} {:answer/id #uuid "356c94ec-fbbf-551d-970e-7d05c53a16e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174730} {:answer/id #uuid "79c9c1e4-dc41-55e4-8853-f413f78e314b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "356c94ec-fbbf-551d-970e-7d05c53a16e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174730} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f5ca45-0719-56a4-9d9b-0c959b47bdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174726}] :question/id #uuid "c7aa456d-0ee8-5696-8091-d1aa1fc2aa26" :question/math-academy-id "q-2409" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^{2} - 2t$. What is the total displacement of the particle between $t = 1$ and $t = 4$?" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f41b1b34-f997-5d61-9acc-0574bcd388cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f41b1b34-f997-5d61-9acc-0574bcd388cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292193} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d45bbc9-97c0-5571-9e73-7d150a54b469" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bc88c45-b00a-5e19-9c3f-9d3e4f3af636" :question/math-academy-id "q-351421" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=6t+2.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=2.$\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(6t+2)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}+2t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[3(2)^{2}+2(2)]-[3(1)^{2}+2(1)] \\\\  & =[12+4]-[3+2] \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca7dee-1a1b-5af6-8719-5d2377004be9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Displacement of a Particle Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f0c57e2-a924-5441-b0e9-63ee697c5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292200} {:answer/id #uuid "e5aee0df-8a58-552d-8045-3f53a3f9792d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f0c57e2-a924-5441-b0e9-63ee697c5134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292200} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7388b20-851e-58e2-85f6-179c4c96a52c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b821d35a-eba2-5209-a10b-30b5c53aa6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-351431" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1}.$ Find the total displacement $d$ of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=5.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t-1})\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =(5-1)^{3/2}-(1-1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089120d4-b37b-5936-8ea4-b68848290606" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle Given a Velocity-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c10a7114-4c5c-5ba6-8bf6-fc3547656242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175063} {:answer/id #uuid "daa08098-ac00-5538-972f-6b7ba2ef4efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175064} {:answer/id #uuid "dae2b7dc-d7df-5e25-b0c2-0e24ef6658df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175065} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9bf632-f90d-5bcc-9f60-de1b0980e1d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175066} {:answer/id #uuid "7031fbf8-174f-5d5a-b871-8522e6511e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c10a7114-4c5c-5ba6-8bf6-fc3547656242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175063} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dadd49d-d78a-5902-a597-96389d3bb02b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175062}] :question/id #uuid "c60ee3fd-371a-5976-8618-8837667483d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-49319" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3576/Source/Images/q-49319.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t = 0$ the particle is located at the position $x = 6$. What is the position of the particle at time $t = 3$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0270f788-1cdb-528c-85e3-c5e8dda56681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292216} {:answer/id #uuid "53db8982-8e73-5277-90aa-165c3b52a5d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292217} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ed76bd-8402-55cd-8079-0e89ab2fe6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292218} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f2dd82-6236-5f56-9bce-6792b0bf2373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292219} {:answer/id #uuid "237c114d-eacb-5eb4-8963-fe8655d0f624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0270f788-1cdb-528c-85e3-c5e8dda56681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292216} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c76b68b-b8cf-58ee-b229-494f9f0c8abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1394edaf-31dd-59b4-a755-2b8323ac548f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81506" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ At time $t=0$ the particle is located at the position $x=3.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{7}{2}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =3+([\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]-[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0-\\frac{\\pi }{6})]) \\\\  & =3+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =3+(0-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"}]}
